%I A397911 #21 Oct 16 2026 15:17:31
%S A397911 109,113,199,283,467,661,1109,1123,1129,1303,1307,1321,1327,1619,1621,
%T A397911 1627,1637,1669,2803,2861,4297,5869,5881,5903,6367,6373,6379,6389,
%U A397911 6397,9479,9497,9551,9859,9871,9883,9887,9901,9907,9929,9931,9941,9949,9973,10333
%N A397911 Primes p such that the sum of the primes <= p exceeds li(p^2), where li is the logarithmic integral.
%C A397911 By the prime number theorem, Sum_{p <= x} p ~ li(x^2), and the difference changes sign infinitely often. The sum is ahead of li(x^2) only rarely: this is the analog, for the weight p, of Chebyshev's bias of pi(x) against li(x), for which no x with pi(x) > li(x) is known.
%C A397911 Assuming the Riemann hypothesis, (Sum_{p <= x} p - li(x^2))*log(x)/x^(3/2) has logarithmic mean -1/3, the contribution of the squares of the primes; for the weight p^k the mean is -1/(2k+1). Assuming in addition the linear independence of the ordinates of the zeros of zeta, the logarithmic density of the set of x with Sum_{p <= x} p > li(x^2) is approximately 0.0605 (computed by the method of Rubinstein and Sarnak).
%C A397911 There are 218324 terms below 10^8 (computed with exact prime sums).
%H A397911 Antonio Mennillo, <a href="/A397911/b397911.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A397911 Antonio Mennillo, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18142838">Cumulative Prime Sums and Riemann Zeros: a fine grid, its ripple, and a family of prime races</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A397911 Michael Rubinstein and Peter Sarnak, <a href="https://projecteuclid.org/euclid.em/1048515870">Chebyshev's bias</a>, Experimental Mathematics, Volume 3, Issue 3, 1994, Pages 173-197.
%e A397911 a(1) = 109: the sum of the primes <= 109 is 1480 > li(109^2) = 1448.42... For the preceding prime 107 the sum is 1371 < li(107^2) = 1402.29..., and no smaller prime has the property.
%t A397911 okQ[p_]:=Total[Prime[Range[PrimePi[p]]]]>LogIntegral[p^2];Select[Prime[Range[1268]],okQ] (* _James C. McMahon_, Oct 16 2026 *)
%o A397911 (Python)
%o A397911 from sympy import nextprime
%o A397911 from mpmath import mp, li
%o A397911 def aupto(N):
%o A397911     mp.dps = 30
%o A397911     out, p, s = [], 2, 2
%o A397911     while p <= N:
%o A397911         if s > li(mp.mpf(p)**2):
%o A397911             out.append(p)
%o A397911         p = nextprime(p)
%o A397911         s += p
%o A397911     return out
%o A397911 print(aupto(10333))
%Y A397911 Cf. A007504, A397910.
